(a) Noise generated from municipally sponsored or approved celebrations or events shall be exempt from the provisions of this chapter.
(b) The following are exempt from the sound level limits of section 27-2:
(1) Sound by public safety vehicles, emergency signaling devices, or authorized public safety personnel for the purpose of alerting persons to the existence of an emergency;
(2) Noise from an exterior burglar alarm of any building, provided such burglar alarm shall terminate its operation within five minutes of its activation if the sound is uninterrupted or ten minutes if intermittent;
(3) Noise from any automobile alarm, provided such alarm shall terminate its operation within five minutes of its activation if the sound is uninterrupted or ten minutes if the sound is intermittent;
(4) The generation of sound in situations within the jurisdiction of the Federal Occupational Safety and Health Administration.
(5) Noise resulting from any practice or performance sponsored by or associated with the educational process administrated by a recognized institution of learning, including, but not limited to, band, choir, and orchestral performances;
(6) Noise that results from the activities of an organized sports league;
(7) Un-amplified bells, chimes or carillons while being used in conjunction with religious services between the hours of 7:00 a.m. — 10:00 p.m.;
(8) Emergency work;
(9) Surface carriers engaged in commerce by railroad;
(10) Events with amplified sound that are operating within the time and volume parameters set forth in an approved special administrative permit.
(Ord. of 5-1-06)

542:
When an officer or inspector discovers litter existing on public or private property, the litter shall be reported to the governing body by such officer or inspector. When such litter is brought to the attention of the governing body by this or any other means, if the governing body deems such litter a nuisance, the governing body shall issue a notice to the parties hereinafter stated and such notice shall:
(1) Be in writing;
(2) Specify the public nuisance and its location;
(3) Request the public nuisance be abated;
(4) Request that the party be and appear at a stated date, time and place for a public hearing to be held on the question of abatement or removal of the litter, if such notice is not complied with;
(5) Be served personally on or be mailed, by certified mail not less than ten days before the hearing, to the owner of the land as shown on the last city ad valorem tax digest or to the occupant if the owner is not in possession of the premises.
(Ord. of 9-11-89, § 4)
